==Faroese==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|fo}} {{term|orð|lang=non}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|fo}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*wurdą}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|fo}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*werdʰo-||word}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|fo}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*wer-||speak}}. Cognate with Old Frisian {{term||word}}, Old Saxon {{term||word|lang=osx}}, Dutch {{term|woord|lang=nl}}, Old High German {{term|wort|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Wort|lang=de}}), Swedish {{term|ord|lang=sv}} and Gothic {{term|sc=Goth|𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳|tr=waurd|lang=got}}.

The Proto-Indo-European root is also the source of Latin {{term|verbum|lang=la}}, Lithuanian {{term|vardas|lang=lt}}, and, more distantly, of Ancient Greek {{term|sc=polytonic|εἴρω|tr=eirō||I say|lang=grc}} and Old Church Slavonic {{term|ротити|ротити сѧ|tr=rotiti sę|to swear|lang=cu|sc=Cyrs}} (Russian {{term|sc=Cyrl|ротиться|tr=rotit’sja||to vow|lang=ru}}).

